1
00:00:00,414 --> 00:00:01,671
Meet Frosty Fusion.

2
00:00:01,986 --> 00:00:04,586
A little ice cream shop with a very loyal
following.

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:06,329
Five years of scooping.

4
00:00:06,586 --> 00:00:07,729
Forty flavors.

5
00:00:07,929 --> 00:00:10,986
And one strawberry that people drive
across town for.

6
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,157
Now, type "best strawberry ice cream" into
Google, anywhere near their shop…

7
00:00:15,529 --> 00:00:17,414
…and there they are. Right at the top.

8
00:00:17,708 --> 00:00:19,537
That spot didn't happen by luck.

9
00:00:19,822 --> 00:00:21,434
It's SEO.

10
00:00:21,484 --> 00:00:25,830
SEO, search engine optimization,
is everything that helps Google decide

11
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:28,394
your page is the best answer to a search.

12
00:00:28,708 --> 00:00:31,565
For Frosty Fusion,
that's five years of it.

13
00:00:32,022 --> 00:00:33,537
Pages Google trusts.

14
00:00:33,594 --> 00:00:35,680
Food blogs that linked to their
strawberry.

15
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:37,737
Reviews that mention them by name.

16
00:00:38,165 --> 00:00:42,880
And all of it is tied to one address:
getfrostyfusion.com.

17
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:45,537
There was one small problem.

18
00:00:46,251 --> 00:00:49,851
Customers kept asking if Frosty Fusion was
a smoothie bar.

19
00:00:50,137 --> 00:00:53,316
One ordered a green detox. Fair.

20
00:00:53,366 --> 00:00:55,994
"Fusion" does sound a little… blended.

21
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:57,566
So, a new name.

22
00:00:57,823 --> 00:00:58,937
Frosty Bites.

23
00:00:59,051 --> 00:01:01,594
Short, sweet, and nobody's ordering kale.

24
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:04,623
New domain: getfrostybites.com.

25
00:01:04,827 --> 00:01:08,398
And then comes the thought that keeps
every business owner up at night.

26
00:01:08,684 --> 00:01:10,627
What happens to the Google spot?

27
00:01:10,913 --> 00:01:12,313
The strawberry page.

28
00:01:12,484 --> 00:01:13,798
The five years.

29
00:01:14,084 --> 00:01:18,577
Change the domain the wrong way,
and Google meets Frosty Bites like a total

30
00:01:18,627 --> 00:01:22,377
stranger. Do it right,
and Google can carry your rankings over to

31
00:01:22,427 --> 00:01:25,113
the new name. You don't start from zero.

32
00:01:25,684 --> 00:01:28,570
So how do you change your domain name
without losing SEO?

33
00:01:28,998 --> 00:01:30,741
It comes down to three steps.

34
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:33,780
First, the thing most people get wrong.

35
00:01:34,065 --> 00:01:35,837
Google doesn't rank your domain.

36
00:01:36,008 --> 00:01:37,351
It ranks your pages.

37
00:01:37,637 --> 00:01:42,037
To Google, Frosty Fusion is a list of
addresses. The homepage.

38
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:43,208
The flavor menu.

39
00:01:43,608 --> 00:01:49,380
And that famous page:
getfrostyfusion.com/flavors/strawberry.

40
00:01:50,037 --> 00:01:51,951
Each page has its own history.

41
00:01:52,237 --> 00:01:54,694
How long it has ranked,
and who links to it.

42
00:01:55,351 --> 00:01:58,473
Change the domain,
and every one of those addresses changes

43
00:01:58,523 --> 00:02:01,265
too. The new ones start with no history.

44
00:02:01,551 --> 00:02:03,494
So the job isn't moving a domain.

45
00:02:03,751 --> 00:02:07,940
It's moving every page,
in a way Google can follow. Three steps.

46
00:02:08,026 --> 00:02:10,712
Map it. Redirect it. Tell Google.

47
00:02:10,982 --> 00:02:12,611
Step one: the map.

48
00:02:12,896 --> 00:02:15,811
Old address on the left,
new address on the right.

49
00:02:16,096 --> 00:02:22,639
getfrostyfusion.com/flavors/strawberry
becomes

50
00:02:22,725 --> 00:02:27,896
getfrostybites.com/flavors/strawberry.

51
00:02:28,696 --> 00:02:32,325
If the paths stay the same,
one rule covers every page.

52
00:02:32,811 --> 00:02:35,896
If some pages changed,
each one gets its own line.

53
00:02:36,182 --> 00:02:41,304
Quick tip: before you change anything,
export your page list from your sitemap or

54
00:02:41,354 --> 00:02:42,668
from Google Search Console.

55
00:02:42,725 --> 00:02:43,868
That's your inventory.

56
00:02:44,168 --> 00:02:47,311
Now, here's where most domain changes
quietly go wrong.

57
00:02:47,768 --> 00:02:50,197
Mapping every page feels like a chore.

58
00:02:50,483 --> 00:02:54,083
So people take a shortcut:
send everything to the new homepage.

59
00:02:54,368 --> 00:02:55,425
And it looks perfect.

60
00:02:55,825 --> 00:02:58,740
Every old link lands somewhere. No errors.

61
00:02:59,025 --> 00:03:02,511
Strawberry fans arrive at
getfrostybites.com.

62
00:03:03,254 --> 00:03:04,854
But they wanted the strawberry.

63
00:03:05,168 --> 00:03:07,168
Now they're on a homepage, hunting for it.

64
00:03:07,454 --> 00:03:08,968
And Google notices too.

65
00:03:09,254 --> 00:03:11,654
Hundreds of pages, all poured into one.

66
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:14,654
It can treat them as pages that no longer
exist.

67
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,597
And that top spot for "best strawberry ice
cream"?

68
00:03:17,883 --> 00:03:19,397
It starts to melt.

69
00:03:20,625 --> 00:03:24,568
One old page,
one matching new page. That's the rule.

70
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:27,095
Step two: the redirects.

71
00:03:27,438 --> 00:03:32,009
Here's what that looks like in RedirHub.
Links. Create.

72
00:03:32,294 --> 00:03:33,609
Website Migration.

73
00:03:33,894 --> 00:03:37,351
Old domain: getfrostyfusion.com.

74
00:03:37,637 --> 00:03:41,037
New domain: getfrostybites.com.

75
00:03:41,323 --> 00:03:42,380
Same paths?

76
00:03:42,666 --> 00:03:46,437
One same-path rule,
and every page forwards to its twin.

77
00:03:46,723 --> 00:03:47,923
Changed pages?

78
00:03:48,209 --> 00:03:52,209
Paste your map,
or upload it as a spreadsheet file, a CSV.

79
00:03:52,494 --> 00:03:56,016
Before anything goes live,
RedirHub shows you what it will create,

80
00:03:56,066 --> 00:03:58,151
and flags every row with a problem.

81
00:03:58,609 --> 00:04:01,294
Nothing is created until you click Create.

82
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:05,180
Every redirect should be a 301,
the permanent kind.

83
00:04:05,523 --> 00:04:09,666
It's the signal Google uses to carry a
page's history to its new address.

84
00:04:09,951 --> 00:04:15,780
Then point getfrostyfusion.com at RedirHub
with the records it gives you.

85
00:04:16,066 --> 00:04:18,216
The padlock is set up automatically.

86
00:04:18,266 --> 00:04:20,009
Our earlier videos walk through it.

87
00:04:20,416 --> 00:04:22,987
Step three: tell Google you've moved.

88
00:04:23,559 --> 00:04:27,044
Google Search Console is Google's free
dashboard for your site.

89
00:04:27,559 --> 00:04:31,651
Open the old site there,
and use Change of Address to point it at

90
00:04:31,701 --> 00:04:36,787
the new one. Then submit the new sitemap:
the list of pages you want Google to find.

91
00:04:37,301 --> 00:04:39,301
And update everything you control.

92
00:04:39,559 --> 00:04:40,959
Your site's own links.

93
00:04:41,016 --> 00:04:42,273
Your Instagram bio.

94
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:44,044
The QR code on the menu.

95
00:04:44,244 --> 00:04:45,701
Your Google Maps listing.

96
00:04:45,873 --> 00:04:48,216
All of it says Frosty Bites now.

97
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:50,461
Now, test it the way Google sees it.

98
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:55,347
Open RedirHub's free Redirect Checker,
and paste the old strawberry page.

99
00:04:55,633 --> 00:04:58,097
One hop. A 301.

100
00:04:58,147 --> 00:05:04,261
And it lands on
getfrostybites.com/flavors/strawberry.

101
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:07,247
That's a pass. Try a few more.

102
00:05:07,504 --> 00:05:08,733
Your best sellers.

103
00:05:09,018 --> 00:05:10,304
An old blog post.

104
00:05:10,590 --> 00:05:12,390
The www version.

105
00:05:12,584 --> 00:05:14,013
And here's the sweet part.

106
00:05:14,470 --> 00:05:19,841
A regular finds a menu from 2021,
the one that still says Frosty Fusion.

107
00:05:20,098 --> 00:05:23,527
She scans it…
and lands right on the strawberry.

108
00:05:23,813 --> 00:05:27,984
Every old link, every old flyer,
still works.

109
00:05:28,724 --> 00:05:30,809
Then comes the part nobody warns you
about.

110
00:05:31,095 --> 00:05:33,381
For a few weeks, your rankings may wobble.

111
00:05:33,667 --> 00:05:37,152
That's Google re-reading your site at its
new address. It's normal.

112
00:05:37,438 --> 00:05:40,467
Whatever you do, don't undo the redirects.

113
00:05:40,752 --> 00:05:44,217
And RedirHub's analytics show how many
people still come in through the old

114
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:46,638
domain, so you can watch the move happen.

115
00:05:46,933 --> 00:05:48,444
Two rules before you go.

116
00:05:48,939 --> 00:05:51,453
Keep the redirects for at least a year.

117
00:05:51,739 --> 00:05:54,596
Google says as long as possible. Honestly?

118
00:05:54,767 --> 00:05:55,767
Keep them for good.

119
00:05:56,282 --> 00:05:59,082
And keep renewing getfrostyfusion.com.

120
00:05:59,425 --> 00:06:01,510
If it expires,
someone else can buy it,

121
00:06:01,625 --> 00:06:03,825
along with every link that still points to
it.

122
00:06:04,110 --> 00:06:07,539
And every strawberry fan who types it. So.

123
00:06:07,796 --> 00:06:09,167
Map every page.

124
00:06:09,396 --> 00:06:14,995
Redirect each one to its twin with a 301.
Tell Google. Then let it settle.

125
00:06:15,384 --> 00:06:19,413
A few weeks later,
search "best strawberry ice cream" again…

126
00:06:19,870 --> 00:06:21,642
…and there's Frosty Bites.

127
00:06:21,813 --> 00:06:23,956
Right where Frosty Fusion used to be.

128
00:06:24,592 --> 00:06:26,677
Same ice cream. New name.

129
00:06:26,963 --> 00:06:28,420
Same spot on Google.

130
00:06:28,706 --> 00:06:31,163
Links to everything in this video are in
the description.

131
00:06:31,449 --> 00:06:34,134
And if you get stuck,
reach out. We're happy to help.

132
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:39,363
Next week: moving hundreds of domains at
once, with one spreadsheet.

